      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the radius of the circle that passes through the points \\((0,4)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) and whose center lies on the x-axis. Let's denote the center of the circle as \\((h, 0)\\) and the radius as \\(r\\).\n\nThe equation of a circle with center \\((h, 0)\\) and radius \\(r\\) is:\n\\[\n(x - h)^2 + y^2 = r^2\n\\]\n\nSince the circle passes through the points \\((0,4)\\) and \\((1,3)\\), we can substitute these points into the circle's equation to get two equations.\n\n1. For the point \\((0,4)\\):\n\\[\n(0 - h)^2 + 4^2 = r^2 \\implies h^2 + 16 = r^2 \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)}\n\\]\n\n2. For the point \\((1,3)\\):\n\\[\n(1 - h)^2 + 3^2 = r^2 \\implies (1 - h)^2 + 9 = r^2 \\quad \\text{(Equation 2)}\n\\]\n\nNext, we will solve these two equations simultaneously to find \\(h\\) and \\(r\\).\n\nFirst, we expand Equation 2:\n\\[\n(1 - h)^2 + 9 = r^2 \\implies 1 - 2h + h^2 + 9 = r^2 \\implies h^2 - 2h + 10 = r^2\n\\]\n\nNow we have two expressions for \\(r^2\\):\n\\[\nr^2 = h^2 + 16\n\\]\n\\[\nr^2 = h^2 - 2h + 10\n\\]\n\nSet these two expressions equal to each other:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 10\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\(h^2\\) from both sides:\n\\[\n16 = -2h + 10\n\\]\n\nSubtract 10 from both sides:\n\\[\n6 = -2h\n\\]\n\nDivide by -2:\n\\[\nh = -3\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(h = -3\\) back into Equation 1 to find \\(r\\):\n\\[\nr^2 = (-3)^2 + 16 \\implies r^2 = 9 + 16 \\implies r^2 = 25\n\\]\n\nThus, the radius \\(r\\) is:\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
