      "text": "To determine the radius of the circle whose center is on the \\(x\\)-axis and passes through the points \\((0,4)\\) and \\((1,3)\\), we start by letting the center of the circle be \\((a,0)\\). The distance from the center to each of the points \\((0,4)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) must be equal to the radius of the circle.\n\nThe distance from the center \\((a,0)\\) to the point \\((0,4)\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(a-0)^2 + (0-4)^2} = \\sqrt{a^2 + 16}\n\\]\nThe distance from the center \\((a,0)\\) to the point \\((1,3)\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(a-1)^2 + (0-3)^2} = \\sqrt{(a-1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\nSince these distances are equal to the radius, we can set them equal to each other:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{a^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{(a-1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\nSquaring both sides to eliminate the square roots, we get:\n\\[\na^2 + 16 = (a-1)^2 + 9\n\\]\nExpanding the right side, we have:\n\\[\na^2 + 16 = a^2 - 2a + 1 + 9\n\\]\nSimplifying the right side, we get:\n\\[\na^2 + 16 = a^2 - 2a + 10\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(a^2\\) from both sides, we get:\n\\[\n16 = -2a + 10\n\\]\nRearranging the terms to solve for \\(a\\), we get:\n\\[\n16 - 10 = -2a\n\\]\n\\[\n6 = -2a\n\\]\n\\[\na = -3\n\\]\nSo, the center of the circle is \\((-3,0)\\). The radius is the distance from the center \\((-3,0)\\) to the point \\((0,4)\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(-3-0)^2 + (0-4)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
